+| 586 | ui.heading |  |  | `app` | Fiction register |
+| 587 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F1 — Contact rehearsal. Supplemental sender/acknowledgement exercise, readback document and its later ground handoff effects. These are performed fictional rehearsals, not astronaut training prerequisites. |
+| 588 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F2 — Recovery rehearsal. Supplemental pickup ownership/readback exercise and the fictional rework it can avoid. Preparation does not move recovery forces closer. |
+| 589 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F3 — Systems rehearsal. Supplemental warning-routing exercise, its readback and its operational handoff effects. Preparation does not add control reserve or identify the hardware fault. |
+| 590 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F4 — Air-support and surface-retrieval reports. Original abstract status reports, now supporting a real operational choice. No claim to historical ship/aircraft positions. |
+| 591 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F5 — Recovery coordination events. The fictional ground ownership clarification, acknowledgement retry and pickup approach consequences. These are explicitly events of the game room, not errors by historical astronauts or operators. |
+| 592 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F6 — Procedure adoption. Two original standing-procedure choices and their reference pages. Neither reverses the operational outcome or the next-mission rehearsal constraint. |
+| 593 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F7 — Alternate return envelope and outcomes. Both selectable opportunities, extra modeled orbit, ordinal critical-reserve warning, earlier sea wait, concentrated later pickup, all six execution/consequence cases, all crew-condition variations, and the six outcome titles. Both routes are authored to be viable in this simplified scenario and both end with the crew alive. There are no numerical chances or hidden survival roll. Actual orbital feasibility, exact fuel quantities, precise recovery time and medical diagnoses are not asserted. Neither branch is labeled the exact historical result. All figures who execute onboard operations remain competent. H6 confirms a historical choice of return revolution; the fictional element is this menu’s specific operational tradeoff and outcomes, not the existence of a timing decision. |
+| 594 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F8 — Named fictional controllers and operational relationships. Mara Voss and Elias Reed, their recommendations, recovery responses, trust-point changes and notes. These are original characters reusing the SYSTEMS and RECOVERY portrait slots. Negative trust never suppresses safety information. The separate user-authorized astronaut relationship branch is F10. |
+| 595 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F9 — Gemini IX-A planning constraints. Compulsory recovery or systems rehearsal following the experienced return risk; the three offered two-exercise plans; branch eligibility; and the player's persisted plan commitment. These are operational preparation consequences in the fictional campaign, not the historical IX-A roster, schedule or training program. |
+| 596 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F10 — Post-flight stance and social consequences. Glen's two statements; the compressed later discussion scene; blaming the crew versus accepting ground contingency responsibility; Armstrong/Scott trust -2 each and Cunningham/Stafford +1 each plus a deepened corps rift on the blame route; Armstrong/Scott +1 each and critics unchanged on the ground route; all associated narration, facts, notes and IX-A relationship displays; and the concrete commissioned docked-contingency task. These are fictional consequences of the player's choice. The ground-side criticism itself is sourced through H5, but its exact implementation and resulting relationship gains are not asserted as historical events. Choosing to blame the crew does not change their demonstrated actions or the source's reported exoneration. Choosing ground responsibility does not erase the existence of disagreement. |
+| 597 | ui.label |  |  | `app` | F11 — Illustrated prologue and recovery presentation. Original staged illustrations, lighting, weather, visual motion, the overview-to-preparation transition, recovery tier labels and result-line summaries. The six underlying game outcomes remain the simplified model described in F7. Different plate lighting expresses tone, not a measured recovery time, storm, medical finding or new consequence. Portrait expressions represent the game relationship changes in F8 and F10; they do not document the real astronauts’ feelings or add their words. |
